  "analysis": [
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Current law taxes personal income at 5 percent of taxable income. The measure increases this rate to 6 percent starting in 2028.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "answer": "The direct cost to taxpayers is the additional 1 percent of their taxable income owed in taxes. Opportunity costs include reduced private consumption and private investment that taxpayers forgo due to having less disposable income. Administrative and compliance costs are essentially unchanged, as the structure of the tax system remains the same.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "Individuals with taxable income pay the higher rate, bearing the direct financial cost. The state government and the individuals or entities that receive state-funded public services benefit from the increased state revenue.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Higher taxes decrease disposable income, which likely leads to reduced consumer spending and personal saving. Some taxpayers, particularly higher earners, might alter their behavior to minimize tax liability, such as shifting or deferring income, adjusting their labor supply, or relocating.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The measure addresses a perceived need for additional state revenue or structural budget balancing by increasing the broad-based personal income tax. The mechanism directly generates more funds for the state. It may create minor perverse incentives by slightly lowering the after-tax return on labor, marginally discouraging work or encouraging tax avoidance.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "answer": "State legislators and the executive branch gain discretion over how to allocate the additional tax revenue generated by the higher rate.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q7",
      "answer": "The macroeconomic effect of state income tax changes is disputed. Some researchers find that raising state income taxes reduces hiring, deters investment, and drives high earners out of state. Other researchers find these negative economic effects are negligible and that the public investments funded by the added revenue can offset any economic drag.",
      "disputed": true
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q8",
      "answer": "Almost nothing is lost in administration; because this is merely a parameter change to an existing tax, the state's revenue department can implement the new rate using current tax collection infrastructure at minimal extra cost.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q9",
      "answer": "The measure acts as a statutory change and can be undone by a subsequent legislative act or voter initiative.",
      "disputed": false
    }
  ],
